1. Junk-pair admission (REFUTED — no junk found). Wrote an independent
   diagnostic test (zz_diag_test.go, not the author's probe) that dumps
   every Resolved/KeptBoth/Unresolved pair's Left/Right date|description|
   amount|Key to a file, run once at window=3 and once at window=5 against
   a fresh copy of the live data dir. `diff` of the two dumps: baseline 24
   lines, after 25 lines, with exactly ONE line added:
   `UNRESOLVED left=2025-11-16|AMAZON MKTPLACE PMTS|-30.81
   right=2025-11-20|Amazon|-30.81 key=63b0e9749caf8b59` — the genuine
   Amazon settlement. Every other line is byte-identical between the two
   runs. This independently corroborates the author's probe.baseline.log /
   probe.after.log (23 kept_winner + 1 kept_both, queue empty → exactly
   the Amazon pair).

2. Decision binding (REFUTED — no rebind). The identity-level diff above is
   the direct test for this: a rebind would show as one existing
   RESOLVED/KEPTBOTH line disappearing and a different UNRESOLVED line
   appearing at the SAME aggregate counts (23/1) the author's probe checks
   only by count. Because the diff shows zero removed/modified lines and
   exactly one addition, no decision silently rebound to a different
   partner. Read loader.go's applyDuplicateDetection (lines 1053-1101):
   `decisions[pair.Key]` lookup means a rebind would in fact demote the
   old decision to `unresolved` and could not net to the same counts by
   coincidence given the full-identity match confirmed above.

3. Mutation adequacy (CONFIRMED). Ran the three committed window tests
   with the constant forced to 4 and to 6 in the private copy:
   - window=4: TestDetect_PendingPosted_WindowBoundaryFiveDays FAILS
     (expects 1 pair at day 5, gets 0) — correctly kills the 4-mutant.
   - window=6: TestDetect_PendingPosted_WindowExceeded FAILS (expects 0
     pairs at day 6, gets 1) — correctly kills the 6-mutant.
   Restored constant to 5 afterward; `git diff --stat` in the copy matched
   the original 4-file/47-line diff exactly before deletion.

4. Full suite: `go build ./...` clean; `go test ./...` → 49/49 packages ok,
   zero FAIL lines — matches the author's fullsuite.log claim.

5. Stale-wording sweep (CONFIRMED DEFECT — grounds for FAIL).
   `internal/services/mcpsvc/admin/duplicates.go` line 83, the
   `list_duplicates` MCP tool's user/agent-facing description, still
   reads: "...a Pending charge that re-appears Posted with a rewritten
   description within 3 days...". This is a plain string literal (not
   derived from `pendingPostedWindowDays`), describing exactly the shape
   ND3 widened, and it is now false: the window is 5 days, not 3. `git
   log -p --follow` on the file confirms it already said "3 days" before
   ND3 (so it was accurate pre-change) and ND3 did not touch this file
   (`git diff origin/master --stat` lists only near_duplicates.go,
   near_duplicates_test.go, .swarm/NEXT.md, .swarm/ledger.tsv). The
   author's own manifest describes rewriting "two doc comments" in
   near_duplicates.go for this exact fact but the sweep instructions for
   this checker explicitly called out "MCP admin tools' descriptions of
   the duplicate shapes ... for stale '3 day' wording shown to users" as
   an attack surface for this task, precisely because it is the same
   fact duplicated across a third surface that the diff did not update.
   No test asserts this string (grep confirms no test file references it).
   This is not a pre-existing/out-of-scope defect: it was accurate before
   this change and became false as a direct, in-scope consequence of it —
   the same "one fact must live in one place, updated everywhere it's
   rendered" failure mode the constitution names for split-classification
   and dual-formatter defects, applied here to a doc string instead of a
   number. An LLM agent calling this tool (or a user reading its output
   via an agent) will be told an incorrect detection window for the
   feature this task just changed.

Net: the core numeric/behavioral claims (genuine settlement detected, zero
junk admitted, zero decisions broken, mutation-pinned constant, full suite
green) all independently verify. The FAIL is narrow and specific to item 5:
a stale "3 days" claim shipped to MCP tool consumers, made false by this
task's own change, in the same file-class (doc-comment-describing-the-
window) the author already edited twice elsewhere but missed here.
